      "text": "To determine the greatest common divisor (GCD) of \\( f(x) = (3x+4)(7x+1)(13x+6)(2x+9) \\) and \\( x \\), we need to find the GCD of the expression without the variable \\( x \\), which we call the constant part of \\( f(x) \\), denoted as \\( c(x) \\). The GCD will be the same for both \\( f(x) \\) and \\( x \\), since the GCD is a divisor of any polynomial evaluated at \\( x \\).\n\nLet's calculate \\( c(x) \\):\n\n\\[ c(x) = (3x+4)(7x+1)(13x+6)(2x+9) \\]\n\nWe only need to find the GCD of the constant parts of the factors when \\( x \\) is a multiple of 15336. If \\( x = 15336k \\) for some integer \\( k \\), then the constant part of each factor is:\n\n\\[ 3x = 3(15336k) = 46008k \\]\n\\[ 7x = 7(15336k) = 107352k \\]\n\\[ 13x = 13(15336k) = 200028k \\]\n\\[ 2x = 2(15336k) = 30672k \\]\n\nNow we need to find the GCD of the numbers 46008, 107352, 200028, and 30672 when \\( k \\) is an integer.\n\nNotice that all these numbers are divisible by 6:\n\n\\[ 46008 = 2^4 \\times 3^4 \\times 7 \\]\n\\[ 107352 = 2^3 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^3 \\times 13 \\]\n\\[ 200028 = 2^2 \\times 3 \\times 7001 \\]\n\\[ 30672 = 2^9 \\times 3 \\]\n\nThe GCD of these numbers is \\( 2^2 \\times 3 \\times 7 = 168 \\).\n\nTherefore, the greatest common divisor of \\( f(x) \\) and \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{168}\\).",
