      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of \\( f(x) = (3x + 4)(7x + 1)(13x + 6)(2x + 9) \\) and \\( x \\), where \\( x \\) is a multiple of \\( 15336 \\), we start by noting that \\( x \\) can be written as \\( x = 15336k \\) for some integer \\( k \\).\n\nFirst, consider the expression \\( 3x + 4 \\). Since \\( x = 15336k \\), we substitute:\n\\[ 3x + 4 = 3(15336k) + 4 = 46008k + 4 = 4k \\cdot 11503 + 4. \\]\nSince \\( 11503 \\) is an odd number, \\( 3x + 4 \\) is not divisible by any even number (since \\( 4k \\) is even for any integer \\( k \\) except \\( k = 0 \\)).\n\nNext, consider the expression \\( 7x + 1 \\). Substituting \\( x = 15336k \\):\n\\[ 7x + 1 = 7(15336k) + 1 = 107352k + 1 = 1 \\cdot 107352k + 1. \\]\nSince \\( 107352k \\) is divisible by 107352 (the smallest common factor of \\( 7x + 1 \\) with \\( x \\)) and 1 is not divisible by any number, we focus on the other factors.\n\nNow, consider the expression \\( 13x + 6 \\). Substituting \\( x = 15336k \\):\n\\[ 13x + 6 = 13(15336k) + 6 = 200316k + 6 = 6k \\cdot 33386 + 6. \\]\nSince \\( 33386 \\) is even, \\( 13x + 6 \\) is not divisible by any odd number (since \\( 6k \\) is even).\n\nLastly, consider the expression \\( 2x + 9 \\). Substituting \\( x = 15336k \\):\n\\[ 2x + 9 = 2(15336k) + 9 = 30672k + 9 = 3k \\cdot 10224 + 9. \\]\nSince \\( 10224 \\) is even, \\( 2x + 9 \\) is not divisible by any odd number (since \\( 3k \\) is a multiple of 3, the only odd divisor would be 3, which contradicts \\( 2x + 9 \\) being sum of even and odd terms).\n\nGiven these substitutions, we see that \\( 3x + 4 \\), \\( 7x + 1 \\), \\( 13x + 6 \\), and \\( 2x + 9 \\) are all forms of expressions that cannot be divisible by any common integer greater than 1 except for 1 itself, and since \\( x \\) is a multiple of \\( 15336 \\), the only common divisor is 1.\n\nThus, the greatest common divisor of \\( f(x) \\) and \\( x \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1} \\]",
